1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,136
Here is a hospital database,
one row per patient.

2
00:00:03,456 --> 00:00:08,043
A researcher asks an innocent question:
how many patients have condition X?

3
00:00:08,363 --> 00:00:10,411
The hospital releases no records.

4
00:00:10,731 --> 00:00:12,331
It just says: forty-one.

5
00:00:12,567 --> 00:00:15,490
A week later,
one new patient, Alice, is admitted.

6
00:00:15,810 --> 00:00:17,282
The researcher asks again.

7
00:00:17,602 --> 00:00:19,287
The answer is now forty-two.

8
00:00:19,533 --> 00:00:23,761
Subtract, and the researcher has learned
something very specific about Alice,

9
00:00:23,761 --> 00:00:25,464
without seeing a single record.

10
00:00:25,784 --> 00:00:28,173
Before we go on, try to fix this yourself.

11
00:00:28,493 --> 00:00:32,717
Pause and ponder: would rounding the count
to the nearest ten protect Alice?

12
00:00:33,037 --> 00:00:35,064
Pause the video if you need more time.

13
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:46,473
Most weeks, yes.

14
00:00:46,793 --> 00:00:50,059
But if the count goes
from forty-four to forty-five,

15
00:00:50,059 --> 00:00:52,925
the rounded answer jumps
from forty to fifty,

16
00:00:52,925 --> 00:00:54,644
and Alice is exposed again.

17
00:00:54,964 --> 00:00:59,231
Any fixed rule that ever changes its answer
has a jump like that somewhere.

18
00:00:59,467 --> 00:01:02,710
So what would it even mean
for a statistic to be private?

19
00:01:03,030 --> 00:01:05,909
And if the fix is to add noise,
how much is enough?

20
00:01:06,150 --> 00:01:10,929
In 2006, Cynthia Dwork, Frank McSherry,
Kobbi Nissim and Adam Smith

21
00:01:10,929 --> 00:01:13,640
answered both questions in this paper:

22
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:17,777
Calibrating Noise to Sensitivity
in Private Data Analysis.

23
00:01:18,097 --> 00:01:21,644
It founded
what we now call differential privacy,

24
00:01:21,644 --> 00:01:24,177
and in 2017 it won the Gödel Prize.

25
00:01:24,417 --> 00:01:28,499
This video covers its three big ideas:
a definition of privacy,

26
00:01:28,499 --> 00:01:33,100
a number called sensitivity,
and a recipe for how much noise is enough.

27
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:37,106
Plus a surprising limit
on what one published table can achieve,

28
00:01:37,106 --> 00:01:39,179
if it must be private in this sense.

29
00:01:40,617 --> 00:01:42,921
First, a map of where this paper comes from.

30
00:01:43,241 --> 00:01:49,278
In 1965, Stanley Warner proposed
randomized response for sensitive surveys.

31
00:01:49,598 --> 00:01:52,329
In a popular version,
you flip a coin in private.

32
00:01:52,649 --> 00:01:54,206
Heads, you answer truthfully.

33
00:01:54,526 --> 00:01:58,003
Tails, you flip again and say yes for heads,
no for tails.

34
00:01:58,323 --> 00:02:00,612
Any single yes could be the coin talking,

35
00:02:00,612 --> 00:02:04,297
yet over thousands of people
the true rate can still be estimated.

36
00:02:04,617 --> 00:02:06,004
Keep this coin in mind.

37
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:07,945
Over the following decades,

38
00:02:07,945 --> 00:02:11,588
statisticians and computer scientists
refined such tricks,

39
00:02:11,588 --> 00:02:16,554
in two flavours: scramble the data going in,
or scramble the answers coming out.

40
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:20,576
Meanwhile, the obvious fix,
just remove the names, kept failing.

41
00:02:20,896 --> 00:02:22,473
Latanya Sweeney showed

42
00:02:22,473 --> 00:02:27,205
that ZIP code, birth date and sex alone
single out most Americans,

43
00:02:27,205 --> 00:02:31,541
and in 1997 she linked
supposedly anonymous hospital records

44
00:02:31,541 --> 00:02:36,042
to a public voter list
and found the governor of Massachusetts.

45
00:02:36,362 --> 00:02:39,647
The lesson:
privacy must be a property of the process,

46
00:02:39,647 --> 00:02:41,862
not of how the released table looks,

47
00:02:41,862 --> 00:02:44,939
and it must hold
whatever else the attacker knows.

48
00:02:45,184 --> 00:02:50,048
Then, in 2003, Irit Dinur and Kobbi Nissim
proved something sobering.

49
00:02:50,368 --> 00:02:52,813
Answer too many questions too accurately,

50
00:02:52,813 --> 00:02:55,855
with errors much smaller
than the square root of n,

51
00:02:55,855 --> 00:02:59,136
and an attacker can rebuild
almost the entire database.

52
00:02:59,456 --> 00:03:02,826
Our subtraction
was the two-question version of this attack.

53
00:03:03,146 --> 00:03:05,685
Noise is the price
of answering many questions.

54
00:03:05,934 --> 00:03:07,237
The hopeful flip side:

55
00:03:07,237 --> 00:03:10,734
with a limited number of questions,
modest noise is enough.

56
00:03:11,054 --> 00:03:15,221
Dwork, Nissim and colleagues built this
into a framework called SuLQ,

57
00:03:15,221 --> 00:03:18,904
for sub-linear queries,
which answers noisy sums like counts.

58
00:03:19,224 --> 00:03:20,974
Remember that limit on questions.

59
00:03:21,294 --> 00:03:23,192
This paper turns it into a budget.

60
00:03:23,434 --> 00:03:25,188
But SuLQ only covered sums,

61
00:03:25,188 --> 00:03:29,023
and its definition tolerated
a tiny chance of a large leak.

62
00:03:29,343 --> 00:03:32,466
This paper takes the leap:
any function of the data,

63
00:03:32,466 --> 00:03:35,828
one clean definition,
and one simple rule for the noise.

64
00:03:36,148 --> 00:03:39,188
Fittingly,
it appeared at a cryptography conference,

65
00:03:39,188 --> 00:03:43,630
where the habit is to define security
against every possible attacker first,

66
00:03:43,630 --> 00:03:44,682
and then prove it.

67
00:03:47,033 --> 00:03:48,164
Here is the setting.

68
00:03:48,484 --> 00:03:53,604
A trusted server, the curator,
holds a database x: n rows, one per person.

69
00:03:53,924 --> 00:03:55,589
An analyst sends a query f,

70
00:03:55,589 --> 00:04:00,089
any function of the database
that returns a number, or a list of numbers.

71
00:04:00,333 --> 00:04:03,747
The curator never releases
the honest answer, f of x.

72
00:04:04,067 --> 00:04:08,675
Instead it draws random noise, Y,
and releases f of x plus Y.

73
00:04:08,995 --> 00:04:13,048
Because the analyst can keep asking,
this is called the interactive setting.

74
00:04:13,368 --> 00:04:16,328
The alternative,
publishing one sanitized table

75
00:04:16,328 --> 00:04:18,659
and walking away, is non-interactive.

76
00:04:18,979 --> 00:04:22,840
At the end, the paper proves
the first is fundamentally more powerful.

77
00:04:23,083 --> 00:04:27,521
So everything hinges on one question:
what distribution should Y have?

78
00:04:27,841 --> 00:04:30,166
Too little noise, and Alice is exposed.

79
00:04:30,486 --> 00:04:32,449
Too much, and the answer is useless.

80
00:04:32,769 --> 00:04:36,673
The title gives the answer:
calibrate the noise to the sensitivity.

81
00:04:36,993 --> 00:04:39,382
But first, what does private actually mean?

82
00:04:40,717 --> 00:04:42,807
The key move is to imagine two worlds.

83
00:04:43,127 --> 00:04:44,983
In one, the database is x.

84
00:04:45,303 --> 00:04:48,951
In the other, it is x prime:
identical except for one row.

85
00:04:49,271 --> 00:04:50,317
Say, Alice's.

86
00:04:50,637 --> 00:04:52,962
Databases like this are called neighbors.

87
00:04:53,282 --> 00:04:57,741
In the hospital, Alice was added;
here, her row just says something different.

88
00:04:58,061 --> 00:05:01,048
Either way,
no single person's row should matter much.

89
00:05:01,284 --> 00:05:02,430
Run the mechanism,

90
00:05:02,430 --> 00:05:05,934
the curator's randomized answering rule,
in each world.

91
00:05:06,254 --> 00:05:10,371
Whatever the analyst gets to see,
the paper calls the transcript;

92
00:05:10,371 --> 00:05:12,334
for now, a single noisy answer.

93
00:05:12,654 --> 00:05:14,588
Because the mechanism is random,

94
00:05:14,588 --> 00:05:18,094
each world gives a whole distribution
of possible outputs.

95
00:05:18,334 --> 00:05:21,811
Pick any output t, and compare
the heights of the two curves there.

96
00:05:22,131 --> 00:05:24,478
Slide t along and watch their ratio.

97
00:05:24,798 --> 00:05:29,042
A private mechanism keeps
that ratio close to one everywhere:

98
00:05:29,042 --> 00:05:33,566
never above e to the epsilon,
never below e to the minus epsilon.

99
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:38,493
That is the paper's definition,
which it calls epsilon-indistinguishability.

100
00:05:38,813 --> 00:05:41,800
The log of the ratio
is the privacy loss at t.

101
00:05:42,120 --> 00:05:45,947
For every pair of neighbors,
every analyst and every output,

102
00:05:45,947 --> 00:05:48,818
it must be at most epsilon
in absolute value.

103
00:05:49,138 --> 00:05:51,553
The absolute value makes it symmetric:

104
00:05:51,553 --> 00:05:54,984
a ratio of two and a ratio of one half
count the same.

105
00:05:55,304 --> 00:05:59,592
For small epsilon,
e to the epsilon is about one plus epsilon.

106
00:05:59,834 --> 00:06:02,074
Let's test it on Warner's coin from our map.

107
00:06:02,394 --> 00:06:03,482
Pause and ponder:

108
00:06:03,482 --> 00:06:07,322
if Alice's true answer is yes,
how likely is she to say yes?

109
00:06:07,642 --> 00:06:08,730
And if it is no?

110
00:06:21,567 --> 00:06:25,116
If the truth is yes,
she says yes three quarters of the time:

111
00:06:25,116 --> 00:06:26,687
heads, or tails then heads.

112
00:06:27,007 --> 00:06:30,783
If it is no, only the second coin
can say yes: one quarter.

113
00:06:31,103 --> 00:06:32,751
The worst ratio is three,

114
00:06:32,751 --> 00:06:37,763
so the coin satisfies the definition
with epsilon equal to the log of three,

115
00:06:37,763 --> 00:06:39,082
about one point one.

116
00:06:39,317 --> 00:06:42,474
Now be the attacker,
trying to tell the two worlds apart.

117
00:06:42,794 --> 00:06:47,311
By Bayes' rule, your new odds
are your old odds times exactly this ratio,

118
00:06:47,311 --> 00:06:50,591
so they move
by at most a factor of e to the epsilon,

119
00:06:50,591 --> 00:06:52,138
whatever you knew before.

120
00:06:52,458 --> 00:06:54,093
Starting from fifty-fifty,

121
00:06:54,093 --> 00:06:58,556
epsilon one tenth leaves you
at most fifty-two and a half percent sure;

122
00:06:58,556 --> 00:07:00,757
epsilon one, at most seventy-three.

123
00:07:01,077 --> 00:07:04,618
Epsilon is set by policy;
the paper calls it the leakage.

124
00:07:04,867 --> 00:07:06,723
Notice what this does not promise.

125
00:07:07,043 --> 00:07:10,300
If a study reveals
that smokers get more heart disease,

126
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:13,912
and Alice smokes,
people may now think she is at higher risk.

127
00:07:14,232 --> 00:07:16,111
That is not a privacy violation:

128
00:07:16,111 --> 00:07:19,577
the same lesson could be learned
from everyone else's data,

129
00:07:19,577 --> 00:07:23,747
so it would happen even if Alice's row
were replaced by someone else's.

130
00:07:25,183 --> 00:07:28,149
Why demand a ratio bound
for every single output?

131
00:07:28,469 --> 00:07:31,407
Cryptography often settles
for something weaker:

132
00:07:31,407 --> 00:07:35,018
statistical distance,
half the area between the two curves.

133
00:07:35,338 --> 00:07:39,903
Equivalently, the most the probability
of any event can differ between the worlds.

134
00:07:40,223 --> 00:07:42,612
The paper shows why that is not enough here.

135
00:07:42,850 --> 00:07:44,353
Consider this mechanism:

136
00:07:44,353 --> 00:07:47,672
pick one row at random and publish it,
word for word.

137
00:07:47,992 --> 00:07:49,568
Change one person's row,

138
00:07:49,568 --> 00:07:53,048
and the output distribution
moves by only one over n.

139
00:07:53,368 --> 00:07:55,197
For a big database that is tiny,

140
00:07:55,197 --> 00:07:57,826
so by the averaged measure
this looks private.

141
00:07:58,066 --> 00:08:00,605
But every output
is somebody's complete record.

142
00:08:00,925 --> 00:08:02,717
The ratio test catches it:

143
00:08:02,717 --> 00:08:08,092
the output showing Alice's real value
has probability one over n in one world,

144
00:08:08,092 --> 00:08:09,608
and zero in the other.

145
00:08:09,928 --> 00:08:11,421
The ratio is infinite.

146
00:08:11,666 --> 00:08:13,996
Averages let catastrophes slip through,

147
00:08:13,996 --> 00:08:16,744
as long as each is rare
for any single person.

148
00:08:17,064 --> 00:08:19,560
A ratio bound rules them out everywhere.

149
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:23,046
It also settles our rounding question:
at the jump,

150
00:08:23,046 --> 00:08:26,125
one world gives that answer
with probability one,

151
00:08:26,125 --> 00:08:28,136
the other with probability zero.

152
00:08:28,456 --> 00:08:31,442
To pass this test,
a mechanism has to be random.

153
00:08:32,883 --> 00:08:34,547
So how much noise do we need?

154
00:08:34,867 --> 00:08:38,869
It depends on a single property
of the query: its sensitivity,

155
00:08:38,869 --> 00:08:42,612
the most that changing one row
can ever change the answer,

156
00:08:42,612 --> 00:08:47,646
over all pairs of neighboring databases,
including ones that do not exist yet.

157
00:08:47,883 --> 00:08:50,660
For a counting query,
like our hospital count,

158
00:08:50,660 --> 00:08:53,195
one row changes the answer by at most one.

159
00:08:53,515 --> 00:08:54,752
Sensitivity one.

160
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:56,057
Now a histogram:

161
00:08:56,057 --> 00:08:59,163
split the possible values of a row
into d bins,

162
00:08:59,163 --> 00:09:01,699
and release how many rows fall in each.

163
00:09:02,019 --> 00:09:05,091
Before I show you, pause:
if Alice's row changes,

164
00:09:05,091 --> 00:09:08,163
how much can the whole histogram
change in total?

165
00:09:08,483 --> 00:09:10,338
Does it depend on the number of bins?

166
00:09:19,183 --> 00:09:21,871
She moves out of one bin and into another:

167
00:09:21,871 --> 00:09:25,135
one count goes down by one,
another goes up by one.

168
00:09:25,455 --> 00:09:26,863
Total change: two.

169
00:09:27,183 --> 00:09:31,646
Adding up absolute changes
across coordinates like this is the L1 norm,

170
00:09:31,646 --> 00:09:35,418
the paper's way to measure sensitivity
for lists of numbers.

171
00:09:35,738 --> 00:09:39,023
And it is two whether there are five bins
or five thousand.

172
00:09:39,343 --> 00:09:41,540
It does not depend on the dimension at all.

173
00:09:41,783 --> 00:09:45,602
Contrast that with:
what is the largest income in the database?

174
00:09:45,922 --> 00:09:48,723
One billionaire can move
that answer by a billion,

175
00:09:48,723 --> 00:09:51,021
and with no cap there is no limit at all.

176
00:09:51,341 --> 00:09:55,095
The standard fix is to cap every value,
say at a million, first.

177
00:09:55,415 --> 00:09:56,550
Remember that trick:

178
00:09:56,550 --> 00:09:59,895
it is exactly what private deep learning
does to gradients.

179
00:10:00,133 --> 00:10:01,456
Two things to remember.

180
00:10:01,776 --> 00:10:04,866
Sensitivity is a property
of the function alone,

181
00:10:04,866 --> 00:10:08,923
not of the particular database,
and it is not chosen by policy.

182
00:10:09,243 --> 00:10:10,651
Epsilon is a choice.

183
00:10:10,971 --> 00:10:12,528
Sensitivity is a fact.

184
00:10:13,967 --> 00:10:15,055
Now for the noise.

185
00:10:15,375 --> 00:10:19,471
The paper uses the Laplace distribution:
symmetric, sharply peaked,

186
00:10:19,471 --> 00:10:23,140
and falling off exponentially
with distance from the center.

187
00:10:23,460 --> 00:10:26,106
Its width is set by a scale parameter,
lambda.

188
00:10:27,150 --> 00:10:28,238
Here is the trick.

189
00:10:28,558 --> 00:10:31,715
Center one Laplace curve at f of x,
forty-one,

190
00:10:31,715 --> 00:10:37,412
and another at f of x prime, forty-two:
the output distributions of our two worlds.

191
00:10:37,650 --> 00:10:40,722
Now plot the log of their ratio
for every output t.

192
00:10:41,042 --> 00:10:43,858
To the left of forty-one,
it is exactly constant.

193
00:10:44,178 --> 00:10:46,460
To the right of forty-two, constant again.

194
00:10:46,780 --> 00:10:48,402
In between, a straight ramp.

195
00:10:48,722 --> 00:10:54,078
It never rises above one over lambda,
and never falls below minus one over lambda:

196
00:10:54,078 --> 00:10:56,168
the shift, divided by the scale.

197
00:10:56,817 --> 00:10:58,750
Why? On a log scale,

198
00:10:58,750 --> 00:11:00,445
a Laplace density is a tent

199
00:11:00,445 --> 00:11:03,835
whose sides have slope one over lambda,
never steeper.

200
00:11:04,155 --> 00:11:05,326
Slide the tent over,

201
00:11:05,326 --> 00:11:09,659
and the gap between the tents
can never exceed the slide times that slope.

202
00:11:09,979 --> 00:11:12,194
That is the whole design principle:

203
00:11:12,194 --> 00:11:15,548
privacy needs noise
whose log density is never steep.

204
00:11:16,284 --> 00:11:21,468
In symbols: two distances to t can differ
by at most the distance between the centers.

205
00:11:21,788 --> 00:11:26,609
So the privacy loss of any output is at most
the sensitivity divided by lambda.

206
00:11:26,929 --> 00:11:32,448
Set lambda to the sensitivity over epsilon,
and the ratio stays within e to the epsilon,

207
00:11:32,448 --> 00:11:35,270
for every output
and every pair of neighbors.

208
00:11:35,590 --> 00:11:37,489
Privacy, proven in one line.

209
00:11:37,734 --> 00:11:42,427
If the query returns several numbers,
add independent Laplace noise to each.

210
00:11:42,747 --> 00:11:46,160
The joint density
then depends on the L1 distance,

211
00:11:46,160 --> 00:11:50,256
which is exactly why sensitivity
is measured in the L1 norm.

212
00:11:50,576 --> 00:11:52,161
That is Proposition 1:

213
00:11:52,161 --> 00:11:56,699
Laplace noise of scale S of f over epsilon
in every coordinate.

214
00:11:56,934 --> 00:11:59,695
Pause and ponder: why not something simpler,

215
00:11:59,695 --> 00:12:03,398
like uniform noise anywhere
between minus ten and plus ten?

216
00:12:14,234 --> 00:12:15,343
Look at the edges.

217
00:12:15,663 --> 00:12:20,601
An output of fifty-one and a half
is possible if the true count is forty-two,

218
00:12:20,601 --> 00:12:22,781
but impossible if it is forty-one:

219
00:12:22,781 --> 00:12:26,564
the same infinite ratio
that sank the random-row mechanism.

220
00:12:26,884 --> 00:12:29,167
Good noise must never rule an output out.

221
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:31,320
And why not the familiar bell curve?

222
00:12:31,640 --> 00:12:35,288
On a log scale a Gaussian is a parabola,
steeper and steeper,

223
00:12:35,288 --> 00:12:38,936
so the gap between two shifted parabolas
grows without bound.

224
00:12:39,256 --> 00:12:43,117
In the tails the ratio explodes,
and no single epsilon works.

225
00:12:43,437 --> 00:12:46,243
Hold on to that Gaussian, though:
it comes back,

226
00:12:46,243 --> 00:12:50,862
and it is the noise you would actually use
to train a neural network privately.

227
00:12:51,100 --> 00:12:53,568
So here is the recipe in the title:

228
00:12:53,568 --> 00:12:57,094
noise scale equals
sensitivity divided by epsilon.

229
00:12:57,414 --> 00:12:59,974
A more sensitive question needs more noise.

230
00:13:00,294 --> 00:13:02,492
Stronger privacy needs more noise.

231
00:13:04,033 --> 00:13:08,471
Back to the hospital, with epsilon one half:
Laplace noise of scale two.

232
00:13:08,791 --> 00:13:12,289
Week one, the curator reports
about forty-three point seven.

233
00:13:12,609 --> 00:13:16,343
Week two, when the true count is forty-two,
about forty point six.

234
00:13:16,663 --> 00:13:17,745
Subtract as before,

235
00:13:17,745 --> 00:13:20,652
and Alice seems
to have lowered the count by three.

236
00:13:20,972 --> 00:13:25,537
A fifty-fifty guess about her can now move
to at most about sixty-two percent.

237
00:13:25,783 --> 00:13:30,114
Now look at what the noise scale depends on:
the sensitivity and epsilon.

238
00:13:30,434 --> 00:13:32,162
Not the size of the database.

239
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:34,423
With a hundred patients,

240
00:13:34,423 --> 00:13:37,800
noise of typical size two
is a few percent of the answer.

241
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:40,254
With a million, it is a rounding error.

242
00:13:40,574 --> 00:13:42,316
Alice is protected by noise

243
00:13:42,316 --> 00:13:45,348
that is large
compared to her own contribution,

244
00:13:45,348 --> 00:13:48,658
which is one,
but tiny compared to the population's.

245
00:13:49,900 --> 00:13:51,522
Privacy for individuals.

246
00:13:51,842 --> 00:13:53,612
Accuracy for populations.

247
00:13:53,932 --> 00:13:55,105
That is the bargain.

248
00:13:55,350 --> 00:13:57,270
Here is one you can answer yourself.

249
00:13:57,590 --> 00:14:01,409
Pause and ponder:
the noise has size about one over epsilon.

250
00:14:01,729 --> 00:14:04,928
What goes wrong
if epsilon is much smaller than one over n?

251
00:14:17,766 --> 00:14:21,094
The noise outgrows n,
bigger than the count could ever be,

252
00:14:21,094 --> 00:14:22,758
and the answer is pure noise.

253
00:14:23,078 --> 00:14:24,828
This is not Laplace's fault.

254
00:14:25,148 --> 00:14:30,016
Any two databases are linked
by a chain of at most n single-row changes,

255
00:14:30,016 --> 00:14:33,802
each changing probabilities
by at most e to the epsilon,

256
00:14:33,802 --> 00:14:37,116
so the ends differ
by at most e to the n epsilon.

257
00:14:37,436 --> 00:14:40,517
If n epsilon is tiny,
every database looks alike,

258
00:14:40,517 --> 00:14:42,214
and nothing can be learned.

259
00:14:42,534 --> 00:14:45,025
This chain trick
is called a hybrid argument,

260
00:14:45,025 --> 00:14:46,630
and it comes back at the end.

